# distort

🔐 simple, state-of-the-art, symmetric encryption

distort is a minimal cli utility for encrypting/decrypting files & data streams of any size.

distort:

- uses the state-of-the-art streaming cipher, xchaha20-poly1305
- seals a stream of any size in 64 KiB chunks, so memory use is constant
- authenticates chunk-by-chunk, failing decryption if stream was reordered, truncated, extended or sealed with another key
- leans on libsodium's excellent [secretstream](https://libsodium.net/api/LibSodium.CryptoSecretStream.html) for implementation 
- Is only 300 lines of C, producing a <80KB binary!

## Installing

### normal installation
distort needs a C compiler, pkg-config and libsodium:

```bash
brew install pkg-config libsodium

# or if using dnf
sudo dnf install pkg-config libsodium-devel

# or if using apt
sudo apt install pkg-config libsodium-dev
```

then run

```bash
./configure
make
make check          # optional
sudo make install
```

### advanced installation
Produce a self-contained, portable binary:

```bash
./configure --enable-static-binary
make

# if running on alpine, you'll also need this before ./configure:
apk add libsodium-static
```

From a fresh git clone/checkout: 

```bash
# ensure you bootstrap before running ./configure
# (needs autoconf, automake, help2man, and git)
./bootstrap
./configure
...
```

## Usage

Either:

1. Bring your own 32 byte key
2. Create one containing exactly 32 high-entropy, random bytes with owner-only access:

```bash
(umask 177; dd if=/dev/urandom bs=32 count=1 > key.key)
```

Encrypt files or piped data:

```bash
# essential usage (file)
distort encrypt -k key.key file.bin > file.bin.enc

# essential usage (stream)
cat file.bin | distort encrypt -k key.key > file.bin.enc

# or compose into larger pipeline, encrypting a full dir and uploading to S3
tar cz sensi \
  | distort encrypt -k key.key \
  | aws s3 cp - s3://archives/sensi.tar.zst
```

Decrypt files or piped data:

```zsh
# essential usage (file)
distort decrypt -k key.key file.bin.enc > file.bin

# essential usage (stream)
cat file.bin.enc | distort decrypt -k key.key > file.bin

# or compose into larger pipeline, streaming file from encrypted s3 archive
set -o pipefail
aws s3 s3://archives/sensi.tar.gz.enc - \
  | distort decrypt -k key.key \
  | tar -O xz projections.csv \
  | column -s, -t
```

## Important

1. You should not trust data produced by `distort decrypt` if the command exited with a non-zero status. This may indicate that one of the segments failed authentication post-decryption.
2. When using `distort decrypt` (or virtually any decryption cli tool) in a pipeline, ensure you are using `set -o pipefail`. If this is not set, a non-zero exit code caused by failed authentication may not propagate back to your shell, giving you a false sense of security.

